        "inputText": "# Bus Reliability Beyond the Average Wait\n\n## Abstract\n\nA timetable can reduce average waiting time while leaving some journeys less reliable. We propose comparing a fixed timetable with a holding policy using an anonymized arrival log from one bus route. The study will examine mean waiting time alongside the frequency of long waits, with peak and off-peak periods reported separately [S2]. A better overall average will not be treated as evidence that all passengers benefit.\n\n## Introduction\n\nPassengers may care as much about an unusually long delay as they do about a typical journey. A single average waiting time can obscure these differences. The available log records scheduled and observed arrivals on weekdays but includes no passenger identities [S1]. While it supports a comparison of service patterns, it cannot establish how individual passengers experienced those patterns.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe data brief limits the available evidence to one route and weekday service [S1]. The experimental brief requires both policies to use the same recorded arrivals [S2]. This comparison will control the input sequence, rather than claim that the historical service operated under both policies. Passenger satisfaction is outside the information recorded in the source packet.\n\n## Methodology\n\nWe will apply the fixed timetable and holding policy to arrivals from 18 weekdays [S2]. Each simulated decision will use only information available at that point in the log. Peak and off-peak periods will remain separate throughout analysis. We will preserve instances of service disruption, as removing difficult days could make the policy appear more dependable than it is.\n\n## Planned Evaluation\n\nThe planned measures include mean waiting time, the frequency of long waits, and the difference between peak and off-peak outcomes. We will report days when the holding policy performs worse as well as those where it improves the average. Sensitivity checks will vary the definition of a long wait without selecting the definition that most favours either policy.\n\n## Limitations and Conclusion\n\nA replay of historical arrivals is a limited test of an operational policy. It does not capture changes in boarding behaviour or traffic caused by a different timetable. Therefore, we will make no causal claim about passenger satisfaction [S2]. The intended result is a clearer comparison of reliability trade-offs on the supplied route, not a general guarantee for all bus services.",
